Approach 1: Brute Force
  Algorithm

In this approach, I first create a separate union sorting.array to store the final result.

I traverse the first sorted sorting.array and insert only those elements that are greater than their previous element. Since the sorting.array is sorted, this removes duplicate values while copying the elements.

After that, I traverse the second sorting.array. For every element, I perform a linear search in the union sorting.array.

If the element is already present, I skip it.
Otherwise, I insert it into the union sorting.array.

This approach works correctly, but every element of the second sorting.array requires searching through the union sorting.array, making it inefficient for larger inputs.

  Time Complexity
Traversing the first sorting.array → O(n)
Traversing the second sorting.array → O(m)
Linear search for every element of the second sorting.array → O(n + m) (maximum size of union sorting.array)

Overall Time Complexity: O((n + m) × m) (approximately O(n × m))

Space Complexity

A separate union sorting.array is used to store the answer.

Space Complexity: O(n + m)

 Approach 2: Better Approach (Binary Search)
 Algorithm

The idea is almost the same as the brute-force approach.

First, copy the unique elements of the first sorting.array into the union sorting.array.

Instead of performing a linear search for every element of the second sorting.array, use Binary Search because the union sorting.array remains sorted.

If Binary Search finds the element, skip it.
Otherwise, insert it into the union sorting.array.

Replacing linear search with binary search reduces the searching time and makes this approach faster than brute force.

 Time Complexity
Traverse first sorting.array → O(n)
Binary Search for every element of second sorting.array → O(m log n)

Overall Time Complexity: O(n + m log n)

 Space Complexity

A separate union sorting.array is required.

Space Complexity: O(n + m)

  Approach 3: Optimal Approach (Two Pointer)
  Algorithm

Since both arrays are already sorted, there is no need to search for elements repeatedly.

Two pointers are used:

first points to the first sorting.array.
second points to the second sorting.array.

Both pointers move simultaneously.

If both elements are equal, insert the value only once into the union sorting.array and move both pointers.
If the first sorting.array contains the smaller element, insert it and move only the first pointer.
If the second sorting.array contains the smaller element, insert it and move only the second pointer.

Before inserting any element, compare it with the last inserted element of the union sorting.array. If both values are the same, skip the insertion to avoid duplicates.

Once one sorting.array is completely traversed, simply traverse the remaining elements of the other sorting.array and continue applying the same duplicate check.

Since each pointer moves only forward and every element is processed only once, this is the most efficient solution.

 Time Complexity

Each element from both arrays is visited only once.

Overall Time Complexity: O(n + m)

  Space Complexity

The union sorting.array stores the final result.

Space Complexity: O(n + m)